{#
  T-4.06a — the shoppable image primitive.

  ONE COMPONENT, TWO PRESENTATIONS. The owner's ruling of 2026-08-06 makes this
  mandatory rather than merely economical: **one hotspot-and-pill component serves
  T-4.06 and T-7.07, and a second implementation is a defect.** The artboards show
  the same marker and the same pill in three places; what differs is only where
  the pills sit — over the image (`overlay`) or stacked beneath it (`list`).

  Usage:
    {% include 'components.ui.hotspot-image' with {
        image:    component.image,
        alt:      component.alt,
        layout:   component.layout,
        hotspots: component.hotspots,
        id:       'lookbook-' ~ position
    } %}

  Parameters:
    image     required — the editorial photograph
    alt       optional — empty means decorative, see below
    layout    'overlay' (pills over the image) or 'list' (pills beneath it)
    hotspots  a list of { x, y, product_id }
    id        unique per instance; `aria-controls` needs it

  COORDINATES ARE PERCENTAGES, AND A PIXEL VALUE ANYWHERE IS A DEFECT. That is
  the ruling and it is also the only thing that works: the image is fluid, so a
  point fixed in pixels drifts off its product the moment the viewport changes.
  They arrive as numbers and are written as `--x`/`--y` custom properties with a
  literal `%` appended here, so no stylesheet has to remember.

  THE PILLS ARE RENDERED BY JAVASCRIPT, AND THE MARKUP HERE IS DELIBERATELY
  EMPTY OF PRODUCT DATA. The customiser has no product picker — the merchant
  types an id — and Twig cannot resolve an id to a product. `salla.product.getDetails()`
  can, at runtime, which is what makes the acceptance criterion true: **a renamed
  or repriced product needs no edit here**, because nothing about it is stored.

  THE NON-VISUAL EQUIVALENT IS THE PILL LIST ITSELF. The criterion asks for one,
  and the artboard already draws it: every hotspot has a pill, every pill names
  its product and its price, and the pills are in the DOM whether or not anyone
  presses a marker. The markers are a shortcut to them, not the only route.
#}
{% set hotspot_layout = layout|default('overlay') %}
{% set hotspot_id = id|default('hotspot-image') %}

<div class="hotspot hotspot--{{ hotspot_layout }}" data-hotspot-root>
    <figure class="hotspot__figure">
        <img class="hotspot__image"
             src="{{ image }}"
             alt="{{ alt|default('') }}"
             width="900"
             height="1200"
             loading="lazy"
             decoding="async">

        {% for point in hotspots %}
            {% set point_id = hotspot_id ~ '-' ~ loop.index %}
            <button type="button"
                    class="hotspot__marker"
                    style="--x: {{ point.x }}%; --y: {{ point.y }}%"
                    data-hotspot-marker
                    data-product-id="{{ point.product_id }}"
                    aria-controls="{{ point_id }}"
                    aria-label="{{ trans('theme.hotspot.marker') }}">
                <span class="hotspot__marker-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </button>
        {% endfor %}
    </figure>

    <ul class="hotspot__pills">
        {% for point in hotspots %}
            <li class="hotspot__pill"
                id="{{ hotspot_id ~ '-' ~ loop.index }}"
                data-hotspot-pill
                data-product-id="{{ point.product_id }}"
                hidden>
                {# Filled by partials/hotspots.js from the product id. Hidden until
                   then, so a failed lookup leaves no empty card behind. #}
            </li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ul>
</div>
